Myth: **The Dance of Dawn**

In the beginning, Day and Night were sworn enemies, each jealous of the other's domain. Day burned with fierce light, while Night embraced absolute darkness. They refused to speak, only pushing each other away at their borders.

The Earth grew weary of their conflict. "Your war brings only harshness," she told them. "Blinding light gives way to impenetrable shadow. My children need gentleness."

Day replied, "I cannot dim my brilliance. It is who I am."

Night added, "And I cannot soften my darkness. We are opposites—forever divided."

The Earth smiled wisely. "Then learn to meet as strangers do—slowly, carefully, with respect."

Reluctantly, they agreed. Day began to approach more gradually, and Night learned to retreat with patience. Where they touched, something miraculous happened: the sky blazed with colors neither had seen before—gold, pink, orange, and purple painted the horizon.

Amazed by this beauty, they began to look forward to their meetings. Now, twice each day, Dawn and Dusk occur when Day and Night embrace at the edges of the world. The colors are their peace offering to each other and their gift to the Earth—proof that even opposites can create something beautiful when they meet with grace.
