"Understanding comes easily when you've already taken what you needed," said the keeper of rose, her voice sad rather than angry.

The amber apprentice worked tirelessly, sharing his secrets, mixing his gold generously into their palettes. But each dawn felt different. The others accepted his contributions cautiously, always watching for the moment he might withdraw again.

The Sun spoke quietly to him one evening: "You cannot force them to trust as they once did. Broken harmony is not the same as unbroken harmony, even when mended."

"Some things, once fractured, bear the mark forever," she continued. "You can act with integrity, but you cannot erase their memory of when you didn't."

The amber apprentice understood: he could become trustworthy again, but trust itself might remain guarded. That distance was not unfair—it was simply the shape of what remained after his choices.

He painted on, hoping actions might speak where words had failed.
